1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,020
It's Sunday.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:05,840
You wake up at eleven.

3
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:06,481
No plans.

4
00:00:06,941 --> 00:00:07,621
No homework due.

5
00:00:08,101 --> 00:00:09,181
Nobody needs you for anything.

6
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:11,782
You move from the bed to the couch.

7
00:00:12,322 --> 00:00:12,722
A show.

8
00:00:13,182 --> 00:00:13,702
Your phone.

9
00:00:14,123 --> 00:00:14,723
Another show.

10
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:15,483
A snack.

11
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:17,203
You never even leave the house.

12
00:00:18,304 --> 00:00:19,164
By six in the evening,

13
00:00:19,184 --> 00:00:20,584
something strange happens.

14
00:00:20,964 --> 00:00:21,945
You feel exhausted.

15
00:00:22,065 --> 00:00:22,385
Heavy.

16
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:23,925
Like you ran a marathon.

17
00:00:24,665 --> 00:00:25,586
But you didn't run anything.

18
00:00:25,626 --> 00:00:26,866
You barely stood up.

19
00:00:27,926 --> 00:00:28,766
Now think about Monday.

20
00:00:29,347 --> 00:00:29,967
School all day.

21
00:00:30,387 --> 00:00:31,628
Then football after class.

22
00:00:31,888 --> 00:00:32,768
You should be wrecked.

23
00:00:33,168 --> 00:00:34,429
And yet, that evening,

24
00:00:34,909 --> 00:00:36,150
you feel more awake.

25
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:38,871
A busy day leaves you buzzing.

26
00:00:39,691 --> 00:00:41,212
An empty day leaves you drained.

27
00:00:42,012 --> 00:00:43,053
That's backwards, isn't it?

28
00:00:43,593 --> 00:00:45,374
If tiredness meant you used up your energy,

29
00:00:45,894 --> 00:00:47,094
resting should fill you up.

30
00:00:47,815 --> 00:00:48,535
So what's going on?

31
00:00:49,055 --> 00:00:50,015
Let's collect some clues.

32
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:51,116
Clue one.

33
00:00:52,036 --> 00:00:53,477
Your brain is about two percent of your

34
00:00:53,517 --> 00:00:53,957
body weight.

35
00:00:54,557 --> 00:00:56,398
But it uses about twenty percent of your

36
00:00:56,438 --> 00:00:57,098
body's energy.

37
00:00:57,579 --> 00:00:58,399
Here's the surprise.

38
00:00:58,799 --> 00:01:00,680
It uses that energy all the time,

39
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:01,460
even at rest.

40
00:01:02,321 --> 00:01:03,741
When you do a hard mental task,

41
00:01:04,041 --> 00:01:04,862
the change is small.

42
00:01:05,562 --> 00:01:07,863
Blood flow to the busy brain areas often

43
00:01:07,903 --> 00:01:09,483
rises by five percent or less.

44
00:01:10,344 --> 00:01:11,985
Lying on the couch doesn't switch your brain

45
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:13,145
into power saving mode.

46
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:14,105
Clue two.

47
00:01:14,846 --> 00:01:16,327
In one study, people did a long,

48
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:17,768
dull monitoring task.

49
00:01:18,148 --> 00:01:20,569
They watched a screen and responded to targets

50
00:01:20,609 --> 00:01:21,270
for over an hour.

51
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:22,771
As time went on,

52
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:23,971
their reactions got slower.

53
00:01:24,412 --> 00:01:25,733
Their heart rate slowed down too.

54
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:27,894
Their bodies were winding down,

55
00:01:28,434 --> 00:01:29,475
not working harder.

56
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:31,956
And when the researchers asked how it felt?

57
00:01:32,757 --> 00:01:34,318
It sounded less like hard work,

58
00:01:34,818 --> 00:01:36,139
and more like boredom.

59
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:37,279
Clue three.

60
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:40,422
In twenty fourteen, psychologists asked people

61
00:01:40,462 --> 00:01:41,663
to do something very simple.

62
00:01:42,463 --> 00:01:43,964
Sit alone in a room for six to

63
00:01:44,024 --> 00:01:44,705
fifteen minutes.

64
00:01:45,325 --> 00:01:45,826
No phone.

65
00:01:46,206 --> 00:01:47,027
Just think.

66
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:49,188
Most people did not enjoy it.

67
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:51,450
In one version, people could press a button

68
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:53,571
to give themselves a mild electric shock.

69
00:01:54,432 --> 00:01:56,053
Twelve out of eighteen men pressed it at

70
00:01:56,073 --> 00:01:56,593
least once.

71
00:01:57,514 --> 00:01:59,195
So did six out of twenty-four women.

72
00:02:00,156 --> 00:02:02,518
Some people chose pain over doing nothing

73
00:02:02,558 --> 00:02:02,818
at all.

74
00:02:03,378 --> 00:02:03,918
Clue four.

75
00:02:04,619 --> 00:02:06,440
Researchers found thirty-six young adults

76
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,362
who felt tired all the time.

77
00:02:09,022 --> 00:02:11,604
They were healthy, but they barely exercised.

78
00:02:12,384 --> 00:02:14,266
Some rode an exercise bike for twenty minutes,

79
00:02:14,606 --> 00:02:15,346
three times a week,

80
00:02:15,607 --> 00:02:16,227
for six weeks.

81
00:02:16,948 --> 00:02:18,148
Some rode at a moderate pace.

82
00:02:18,829 --> 00:02:20,470
Some rode at a very easy pace.

83
00:02:21,151 --> 00:02:22,692
And some didn't exercise at all.

84
00:02:23,432 --> 00:02:25,394
Both groups who rode felt more energetic.

85
00:02:26,094 --> 00:02:28,075
The easy riders felt the biggest drop in

86
00:02:28,135 --> 00:02:28,576
fatigue.

87
00:02:29,356 --> 00:02:30,917
And it wasn't because they got fitter.

88
00:02:31,638 --> 00:02:33,940
Their fitness change didn't explain it.

89
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:35,781
So here's the question.

90
00:02:36,281 --> 00:02:37,763
Why do you feel tired after a day

91
00:02:37,803 --> 00:02:38,544
of doing nothing?

92
00:02:39,204 --> 00:02:39,625
Is it A.

93
00:02:39,725 --> 00:02:41,487
Your body still burned through its energy,

94
00:02:41,827 --> 00:02:43,068
and your battery ran low?

95
00:02:43,709 --> 00:02:43,949
B.

96
00:02:44,369 --> 00:02:45,791
Tiredness isn't a battery.

97
00:02:46,291 --> 00:02:47,693
It's a signal your brain sends,

98
00:02:47,833 --> 00:02:49,475
and an empty day sets it off?

99
00:02:50,536 --> 00:02:51,517
Or C.

100
00:02:52,037 --> 00:02:52,858
You slept too much,

101
00:02:53,359 --> 00:02:54,760
and extra sleep makes you tired?

102
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:56,608
Pause the video and take a guess.

103
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:06,581
Here's the key idea.

104
00:03:07,142 --> 00:03:07,882
Think about hunger.

105
00:03:08,443 --> 00:03:09,984
You don't feel hungry only when your body

106
00:03:10,045 --> 00:03:10,765
runs out of fuel.

107
00:03:11,346 --> 00:03:13,348
You feel hungry when you smell pizza.

108
00:03:13,908 --> 00:03:14,789
Or when it's lunchtime.

109
00:03:15,610 --> 00:03:17,252
Hunger is a message your brain creates to

110
00:03:17,332 --> 00:03:18,153
steer what you do.

111
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:20,916
Many researchers think tiredness works in

112
00:03:20,956 --> 00:03:21,616
a similar way.

113
00:03:22,217 --> 00:03:23,178
It's not a fuel gauge.

114
00:03:23,558 --> 00:03:24,179
It's a signal.

115
00:03:24,699 --> 00:03:25,780
Start with the battery idea.

116
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:28,162
If your brain were a phone battery,

117
00:03:28,522 --> 00:03:29,863
a lazy day would charge it up.

118
00:03:30,563 --> 00:03:32,365
But your brain uses roughly the same energy

119
00:03:32,405 --> 00:03:34,526
whether you're solving puzzles or staring

120
00:03:34,566 --> 00:03:35,047
at a wall.

121
00:03:35,807 --> 00:03:37,148
So a day on the couch doesn't save

122
00:03:37,188 --> 00:03:37,689
much fuel.

123
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:40,171
And a busy day doesn't drain much extra.

124
00:03:40,851 --> 00:03:42,732
The feeling of tiredness must be coming from

125
00:03:42,853 --> 00:03:43,353
somewhere else.

126
00:03:44,013 --> 00:03:45,434
One influential idea goes like this.

127
00:03:46,014 --> 00:03:46,934
Your brain keeps asking:

128
00:03:47,415 --> 00:03:48,975
is this the best use of my time

129
00:03:49,015 --> 00:03:49,396
right now?

130
00:03:50,176 --> 00:03:51,217
When the answer is no,

131
00:03:51,637 --> 00:03:53,418
you start to feel effort and fatigue.

132
00:03:54,018 --> 00:03:55,759
It's your brain nudging you to switch to

133
00:03:55,819 --> 00:03:56,339
something else.

134
00:03:57,099 --> 00:03:59,120
Think of a boring class versus a game

135
00:03:59,160 --> 00:03:59,621
you love.

136
00:04:00,221 --> 00:04:01,421
Same hour, same brain.

137
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:02,842
One feels endless.

138
00:04:03,442 --> 00:04:04,343
The other flies by.

139
00:04:05,203 --> 00:04:06,404
This is still a hypothesis,

140
00:04:06,444 --> 00:04:07,724
and scientists debate it.

141
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:09,345
But it explains a lot.

142
00:04:10,066 --> 00:04:11,646
Tiredness can be about value,

143
00:04:12,147 --> 00:04:12,847
not just fuel.

144
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:14,709
Now add boredom.

145
00:04:15,490 --> 00:04:17,352
Psychologists describe boredom as a signal

146
00:04:17,372 --> 00:04:17,613
too.

147
00:04:18,334 --> 00:04:19,776
It appears when your attention can't really

148
00:04:19,836 --> 00:04:20,817
grab onto anything.

149
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:23,080
That can happen when there's too little going

150
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:24,522
on, or too much.

151
00:04:25,123 --> 00:04:26,845
It also appears when what you're doing doesn't

152
00:04:26,865 --> 00:04:27,887
feel meaningful to you.

153
00:04:28,507 --> 00:04:30,029
And boredom often feels a lot like being

154
00:04:30,089 --> 00:04:30,410
tired.

155
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:31,871
Here's the sneaky part.

156
00:04:32,471 --> 00:04:33,992
A day on your phone doesn't feel empty.

157
00:04:34,213 --> 00:04:35,674
It's full of videos and noise.

158
00:04:36,254 --> 00:04:37,876
But lots of input isn't the same as

159
00:04:37,916 --> 00:04:38,656
being engaged.

160
00:04:39,457 --> 00:04:41,578
Clip after clip, your attention never really

161
00:04:41,639 --> 00:04:41,959
settles.

162
00:04:42,619 --> 00:04:44,281
And very little of it matters to you.

163
00:04:44,841 --> 00:04:46,683
So your brain lights up both warning lights

164
00:04:46,723 --> 00:04:47,063
at once.

165
00:04:47,783 --> 00:04:49,485
Remember the dull monitoring task?

166
00:04:50,266 --> 00:04:51,588
When nothing interesting happens,

167
00:04:51,988 --> 00:04:53,530
your body seems to power down.

168
00:04:53,931 --> 00:04:54,752
Your heart slows.

169
00:04:55,273 --> 00:04:56,634
You feel heavy and sleepy.

170
00:04:57,255 --> 00:04:58,296
Not because you worked hard.

171
00:04:58,657 --> 00:05:01,160
Because almost nothing asked you to wake up.

172
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:03,362
And remember the people who couldn't sit still?

173
00:05:04,302 --> 00:05:06,064
The main finding was later tested with over

174
00:05:06,184 --> 00:05:08,166
two and a half thousand people in eleven

175
00:05:08,206 --> 00:05:08,566
countries.

176
00:05:09,287 --> 00:05:11,349
In every country, people enjoyed doing something

177
00:05:11,409 --> 00:05:13,171
more than just sitting with their thoughts.

178
00:05:14,051 --> 00:05:16,333
So doing nothing isn't automatically restful.

179
00:05:16,934 --> 00:05:17,515
For most of us,

180
00:05:17,595 --> 00:05:18,996
it's actually kind of unpleasant.

181
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:20,237
Now the bike clue.

182
00:05:20,877 --> 00:05:22,478
One small study is easy to doubt.

183
00:05:22,558 --> 00:05:23,319
But it's not alone.

184
00:05:23,899 --> 00:05:24,640
In twenty oh six,

185
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:27,322
researchers combined seventy trials with nearly

186
00:05:27,562 --> 00:05:28,823
seven thousand people.

187
00:05:29,663 --> 00:05:31,825
Regular exercise made people feel more energetic

188
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:32,645
and less tired.

189
00:05:33,426 --> 00:05:35,007
A newer review in twenty twenty-two,

190
00:05:35,367 --> 00:05:37,929
with eighty-one studies, found the same pattern.

191
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:40,191
The effect is small to moderate.

192
00:05:40,311 --> 00:05:41,152
It's not magic.

193
00:05:41,632 --> 00:05:43,053
But moving seems to tell your brain that

194
00:05:43,073 --> 00:05:44,534
the day is worth being awake for.

195
00:05:45,114 --> 00:05:45,934
There's one more piece.

196
00:05:46,475 --> 00:05:47,335
Your body clock.

197
00:05:47,975 --> 00:05:49,756
On school days, you sleep from eleven to

198
00:05:49,816 --> 00:05:50,096
seven.

199
00:05:50,516 --> 00:05:52,757
On Sunday, you sleep from two to eleven.

200
00:05:53,477 --> 00:05:55,178
That's like flying to another time zone for

201
00:05:55,198 --> 00:05:55,678
the weekend.

202
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:57,338
Then on Monday morning,

203
00:05:57,798 --> 00:05:58,819
you fly right back.

204
00:05:59,319 --> 00:06:01,260
Scientists call it social jetlag.

205
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,721
In surveys, people with more of it tend

206
00:06:03,761 --> 00:06:05,681
to sleep worse and feel worse.

207
00:06:06,502 --> 00:06:08,002
So the problem isn't sleeping too much.

208
00:06:08,522 --> 00:06:10,443
It's sleeping at the wrong time for your

209
00:06:10,483 --> 00:06:10,743
body.

210
00:06:11,303 --> 00:06:12,285
And while you lie around,

211
00:06:12,345 --> 00:06:13,426
something else keeps happening.

212
00:06:13,927 --> 00:06:14,908
From the moment you wake up,

213
00:06:15,429 --> 00:06:17,712
pressure to sleep slowly builds in your brain.

214
00:06:18,493 --> 00:06:19,935
It rises with time awake,

215
00:06:19,995 --> 00:06:20,676
whatever you do.

216
00:06:21,397 --> 00:06:22,859
A lazy day doesn't pause it.

217
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:25,541
To be fair, not every tired Sunday is

218
00:06:25,561 --> 00:06:26,181
about boredom.

219
00:06:26,761 --> 00:06:27,942
If you slept badly all week,

220
00:06:28,282 --> 00:06:30,063
your body may simply need sleep.

221
00:06:30,763 --> 00:06:31,523
If you're getting sick,

222
00:06:31,944 --> 00:06:33,624
tiredness is one of the first signs.

223
00:06:34,245 --> 00:06:35,785
And feeling flat for a long time can

224
00:06:35,805 --> 00:06:36,846
be part of low mood.

225
00:06:37,546 --> 00:06:38,967
Those are real causes too.

226
00:06:39,787 --> 00:06:41,828
Rest and care are the right answer there.

227
00:06:42,588 --> 00:06:43,589
But if you slept fine,

228
00:06:44,089 --> 00:06:46,030
and you're healthy, and you still feel drained

229
00:06:46,070 --> 00:06:46,830
after doing nothing?

230
00:06:47,650 --> 00:06:49,231
That's usually the signal talking.

231
00:06:49,871 --> 00:06:51,112
So, the answer is B.

232
00:06:51,952 --> 00:06:53,693
Tiredness isn't a battery reading.

233
00:06:53,753 --> 00:06:55,775
It's a signal your brain creates.

234
00:06:56,535 --> 00:06:58,236
An empty day hits every switch at once.

235
00:06:58,857 --> 00:06:59,317
Boredom.

236
00:06:59,577 --> 00:07:00,398
Hardly any movement.

237
00:07:00,818 --> 00:07:02,479
And a body clock knocked off schedule.

238
00:07:03,399 --> 00:07:05,901
A is wrong, because lying still saves almost

239
00:07:06,101 --> 00:07:06,761
no energy.

240
00:07:07,362 --> 00:07:08,482
And C is only half right.

241
00:07:08,983 --> 00:07:10,183
It's not the amount of sleep.

242
00:07:10,223 --> 00:07:11,104
It's the timing.

243
00:07:11,704 --> 00:07:13,366
So how do you rest without feeling drained?

244
00:07:14,226 --> 00:07:16,728
First, think of rest as switching,

245
00:07:17,109 --> 00:07:18,230
not shutting down.

246
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:20,472
Pick something that grabs your attention and

247
00:07:20,492 --> 00:07:21,292
means something to you.

248
00:07:21,973 --> 00:07:23,874
Drawing, cooking, a call with a friend.

249
00:07:24,395 --> 00:07:25,536
Not just more scrolling.

250
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:27,257
Second, move a little.

251
00:07:28,038 --> 00:07:29,819
A walk or an easy bike ride counts.

252
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:32,482
In that study, easy was enough.

253
00:07:33,122 --> 00:07:34,884
Third, try to wake up close to your

254
00:07:34,964 --> 00:07:36,385
usual time, even on weekends.

255
00:07:36,945 --> 00:07:38,266
Next Sunday, you wake up at nine,

256
00:07:38,546 --> 00:07:39,046
not eleven.

257
00:07:39,546 --> 00:07:40,527
You go for a short walk.

258
00:07:41,047 --> 00:07:42,868
You spend an hour building something you care

259
00:07:42,908 --> 00:07:43,128
about.

260
00:07:43,688 --> 00:07:44,549
You still watch a show.

261
00:07:44,729 --> 00:07:45,689
You still check your phone.

262
00:07:46,610 --> 00:07:47,771
But at six in the evening,

263
00:07:48,071 --> 00:07:48,811
you notice something.

264
00:07:49,351 --> 00:07:50,172
You're not exhausted.

265
00:07:50,192 --> 00:07:51,232
You actually feel awake.

266
00:07:51,833 --> 00:07:52,953
You didn't need more rest.

267
00:07:53,173 --> 00:07:54,514
You needed a better kind.

268
00:07:55,094 --> 00:07:56,256
If you feel tired for weeks,

269
00:07:56,477 --> 00:07:57,860
even with enough sleep and rest,

270
00:07:58,481 --> 00:07:59,864
it's worth talking to a doctor.

271
00:08:00,445 --> 00:08:02,308
Sometimes tiredness is your brain's message.

272
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:04,012
Sometimes it's your body's.
